WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.713
A solar plant in the desert loses output to sand and

2
00:00:02.813 --> 00:00:06.121
dust. The question this model asks is narrower and more useful.

3
00:00:06.221 --> 00:00:09.042
Which of that can a crust on the ground actually stop?

4
00:00:09.833 --> 00:00:13.166
Start with the wind. We take three years of hourly wind and fit

5
00:00:13.266 --> 00:00:16.762
the spread of speeds. The reason we keep the whole spread, not the

6
00:00:16.862 --> 00:00:20.413
average, is that sand flux climbs as roughly the cube of wind speed

7
00:00:20.513 --> 00:00:23.682
above a threshold. A few strong days do almost all the work.

8
00:00:24.666 --> 00:00:26.856
Only this shaded tail, the hours above

9
00:00:26.956 --> 00:00:29.085
the threshold, moves any sand at all.

10
00:00:29.866 --> 00:00:32.782
Next, direction. We sum the drift over sixteen compass

11
00:00:32.882 --> 00:00:35.910
sectors, the Fryberger method, to get the direction sand

12
00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:38.758
actually moves and how one-sided it is. That points

13
00:00:38.858 --> 00:00:41.495
straight at the edge of the plant worth treating.

14
00:00:43.266 --> 00:00:46.354
Now the key split. Sand near the ground hops in short

15
00:00:46.454 --> 00:00:49.482
arcs, metres at a time. That is saltation, and it is

16
00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:52.309
exactly what a crust on the surface holds down.

17
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.044
The fine dust is a different animal. It rides the

18
00:00:55.144 --> 00:00:58.404
wind hundreds of kilometres from sources over Iraq and Iran.

19
00:00:58.504 --> 00:01:00.476
No crust on a UAE plant touches that.

20
00:01:01.266 --> 00:01:03.450
So the crust addresses sand piling up and

21
00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:05.790
burying things, not the haze on the glass.

22
00:01:05.866 --> 00:01:09.137
And the honest weak link is the last step: turning blowing sand

23
00:01:09.237 --> 00:01:12.989
into lost power. We have no measured coefficient for glass abrasion yet,

24
00:01:13.089 --> 00:01:15.771
so we leave that number blank rather than invent it.

